    // ---- phase 5: findings & recommendations ----
    "p5.callout.q": "The central question this phase exists to answer",
    "p5.callout.a": "Anyone can run a pivot table and see that most applications get approved, or that California and Texas have the most volume. That's obvious from the raw file. What follows is <b>not</b> — each finding below required combining segmentation, pattern mining, and anomaly detection, then checking them against each other.",
    "p5.finding.1": "<b>Purchased loans are structurally missing demographic data</b> — found independently by K-Means, Apriori (lift 11.0–12.5×, confidence 87–97%), and the rule network. Three unrelated methods agreeing makes this a discovery, not a data quirk.",
    "p5.finding.2": "<b>Many \"extreme\" anomalies were missing-data artifacts, not real outliers.</b> 71.5% of the strictest (all-3-methods) flagged anomalies were the imputed-mean placeholder, found by cross-checking Phase 1's imputation against Phase 4's output — correcting two Phase 2 segment profiles.",
    "p5.finding.3": "<b>Withdrawn applications carry the same missing-demographics signature as purchased loans, for a different reason</b> — the highest-lift rule found anywhere (13.22×), pointing to a disclosure-process friction point, not the secondary-market pattern.",
    "p5.finding.4": "<b>The applicant population has no natural density-based clusters — it's a continuum.</b> DBSCAN (ε = 5.7) found 99.4% of applicants form one connected mass; only 0.6% are genuinely isolated. K-Means' 4 segments are a useful <i>imposed</i> grouping, not a structure the data was \"waiting to reveal.\"",
    "p5.rec.1": "<b>Exclude or separately flag purchased loans</b> before computing any fair-lending or demographic-outcome metric — their missing data will otherwise silently distort the results.",
